+# M1 plan · Dev loop (2026-10-19)
+
+Goal (spec §5 M1): a session can launch a Mixxx safely, load the mapping, feed it MIDI and read the result,
+without touching Michael's real profile, the speakers or the Rane One. This plan settles every choice M1-02..M1-05
+need; D9 in `docs/decisions.md` records the defaults. Build-only policy: the stubbed tests are what a session must
+make pass. The live steps (null sink, Mixxx start) are written out here and run only where the feature steps ask.
+
+Source facts below cite the Mixxx **2.5** branch (`https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mixxxdj/mixxx/2.5/<path>`),
+read 2026-10-19 and checked by a second reviewer the same day. Machine facts were read on Michael's desktop the
+same day (Flatpak 1.18.1, `org.mixxx.Mixxx` 2.5.6 on `org.kde.Platform` 6.11, `pactl` 16.1 on PipeWire 1.5.85,
+Node 24.21, COSMIC on Wayland). Anything marked *expected* is confirmed by the session named next to it.
+
+Paths used throughout:
+
+| Name | Path |
+|---|---|
+| `$PROFILE` | `~/.var/app/org.mixxx.Mixxx/dev-profile/` (D3). Override: `MIXXX_DEV_PROFILE` (tests point it at a temp dir). |
+| Real profile, never touched | `~/.var/app/org.mixxx.Mixxx/.mixxx/` (the Flatpak's `persistent=.mixxx`) |
+| Scratch | `/tmp/harness/rane-one-mixxx/<run>/` |
+
+**Profile guard (every tool that takes `$PROFILE`):** resolve it with `realpath -m`; **refuse with exit 3** when it
+is the real profile, inside it, or an ancestor of it (e.g. `~/.var/app/org.mixxx.Mixxx` itself), also through a
+symlink. `tools/mixxx-dev start` additionally refuses (exit 3) a live profile outside `~/.var/app/org.mixxx.Mixxx/`
+(the sandbox could not see it) unless `flatpak` is the test stub. Usage errors exit 2, like `tools/midi-send`.
+
+## Null sink
+
+D7 says unattended audio goes to a PipeWire null sink and the default sink never changes. The default sink today
+is the Rane One itself (`alsa_output.usb-Rane_Rane_ONE_A-00.analog-surround-40`), so a Mixxx stream that is not
+routed explicitly, or whose sink disappears, plays out of the controller. Routing is therefore checked, not assumed.
+
+- **Name:** `mixxx-dev`. **Rate:** 48 kHz, 2 channels (the One is 48 kHz only; one rate everywhere avoids
+  resampling surprises when M3 records the sink's monitor).
+- **Create:** `tools/null-sink up` runs
+  `pactl load-module module-null-sink sink_name=mixxx-dev rate=48000 channels=2 sink_properties=device.description=mixxx-dev`
+  and writes the module id `pactl` prints to `$PROFILE/null-sink.module`. If `pactl list short sinks` already
+  lists `mixxx-dev`, `up` does nothing (no second module) and exits 0.
+- **Remove:** `tools/null-sink down` **refuses (exit 1) while any stream is on `mixxx-dev`** (`pactl list
+  sink-inputs` shows a `Sink:` equal to its index) or while `tools/mixxx-dev status` says running: PipeWire moves
+  the streams of a removed sink to the default sink, i.e. to the Rane One. Otherwise it unloads **only** the id in
+  `null-sink.module`, and only if `pactl list short modules` still shows that id as `module-null-sink` with
+  `sink_name=mixxx-dev`; then it deletes the file. No file: exit 0, nothing unloaded. It never unloads by name.
+  Order at the end of a session: `tools/mixxx-dev stop`, then `tools/null-sink down`.
+- **Status:** `tools/null-sink status` prints `mixxx-dev` and the module id from `null-sink.module`, or
+  `mixxx-dev (not ours)` when the sink exists without that file; exit 0. Otherwise `absent`, exit 1.
+- **Never** `pactl set-default-sink`, `pactl set-default-source`, `wpctl set-default` or `pw-metadata` writes. The
+  tests' `pactl` stub exits 99 on `set-default-*`. `up` also compares `pactl info | grep 'Default Sink'` before
+  and after loading; if it changed, it unloads its module at once and exits 1.
+- **Routing method: `PULSE_SINK=mixxx-dev`, passed with `flatpak run --env=PULSE_SINK=mixxx-dev`.** Inside the
+  sandbox Mixxx plays through PortAudio's ALSA API to the ALSA `pulse` device, a libpulse client that honours
+  `PULSE_SINK`. The launch also passes `--env=PULSE_PROP_OVERRIDE=application.name=mixxx-dev` so the dev
+  Mixxx's stream can be told apart from a Mixxx Michael runs (libpulse applies `PULSE_PROP_OVERRIDE` to the
+  client's property list; *expected*, M1-04).
+- **The check (`tools/mixxx-dev start`, after launch):** poll `pactl list sink-inputs` once a second, for at most
+  30 s counted from launch, until a stream with `application.name = "mixxx-dev"` appears. PASS only if every such
+  stream has `Sink:` equal to the index of `mixxx-dev` in `pactl list short sinks`. Other Mixxx streams (not
+  `mixxx-dev`) are ignored, not counted. No stream after 30 s, a stream on any other sink, or any `pactl` error
+  is a FAIL: `start` **stops its Mixxx immediately** and exits 1. A second, independent check: the Rane ONE's
+  playback substreams in `/proc/asound/card<n>/pcm*p/sub*/status` (card resolved by name from
+  `/proc/asound/cards`, read-only) must not have an `owner_pid` belonging to the dev Mixxx (the Flatpak has
+  `devices=all`, so Mixxx could open the hardware PCM directly, which `pactl` would not see). A fresh dev Mixxx
+  has no track loaded and plays silence, so the at most 30 s before a failed check is caught carries no audio.
+- **Long runs** (M3 recordings, replays over a minute) repeat the check every 10 s and stop Mixxx on a FAIL.
+- Fallback, used only if the check fails in M1-04: the runtime's `99-pulseaudio-default.conf` makes ALSA
+  `default` a pulse PCM too, so the seed's device can become `default` with the same `PULSE_SINK` routing. The
+  `pipewire` ALSA PCM ignores `PULSE_SINK` and is not used. Whatever M1-04 ends up using goes under Evidence and
+  into D9.
+
+## Dev profile seed
+
+Mixxx blocks startup on modal dialogs on a fresh profile: the music-library chooser, the sound-device error /
+"no output" dialog, the menu-bar hint (`alwaysHideMenuBarDlg()`, shown on Linux without a global menu bar, i.e.
+COSMIC, before controllers are set up: `src/mixxxmainwindow.cpp`), and the OpenGL direct-rendering warning
+(`checkDirectRendering()`). `tools/mixxx-dev start` writes the seed before **every** launch:
+
+| File | Owner | Content | Why |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| `$PROFILE/mixxx.cfg` | Mixxx; launcher sets the keys below on every start | see below | config version, binding, no dialogs |
+| `$PROFILE/soundconfig.xml` | **launcher**: rewritten on every start | see below | 48 kHz, Main out to the `pulse` device |
+| `$PROFILE/mixxxdb.sqlite` | Mixxx; created by the launcher if missing | table `directories` with one row: the absolute path of `$PROFILE/music` | suppresses the library chooser (see below) |
+| `$PROFILE/music/` | launcher | empty directory | the library root, under the dev profile |
+| `$PROFILE/controllers/` | `tools/install-mapping` | the mapping files | user mapping path Mixxx searches |
+
+**Library directory: not a mixxx.cfg key in 2.5.** `src/coreservices.cpp` shows the chooser
+(`QFileDialog::getExistingDirectory`, "Choose music library directory") when
+`internalCollection()->loadRootDirs().isEmpty()`, and root dirs live in the database table `directories`
+(`res/schema.xml` revision 23, `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS directories (directory TEXT UNIQUE)`). So `mixxx.cfg`
+alone cannot stop the chooser. When `mixxxdb.sqlite` is missing, the launcher creates it with Node's built-in
+`node:sqlite` (zero dependencies, D5): `CREATE TABLE directories (directory TEXT UNIQUE); INSERT INTO directories
+VALUES (?)`. Mixxx's `SchemaManager` reads a missing `settings` table as version 0 and applies every revision;
+revision 1 creates `settings` with `IF NOT EXISTS`, revision 23 is `IF NOT EXISTS` and nothing later touches
+`directories`, so the seeded row survives. If M1-04 shows otherwise (schema error in the log, or the chooser),
+the fallback is a bootstrap run: start Mixxx, wait until `mixxxdb.sqlite` has `settings.mixxx.schema.version`,
+stop it, insert the row, start again. The path is the same inside and outside the sandbox (`HOME` is the real home
+inside; the initializer listed `~/.var/app/org.mixxx.Mixxx` from inside).
+
+`mixxx.cfg` (Mixxx's format: `[Group]` line, then `Key Value` lines, blank line between groups). The launcher
+creates the file if missing and, on every start, sets exactly these keys (adding or replacing them, leaving every
+other line as Mixxx wrote it):
+
+```
+[Config]
+Version 2.5.6
+show_menubar_hint 0
+
+[Controller]
+Midi_Through_Port-0 1
+
+[ControllerPreset]
+Midi_Through_Port-0 <absolute $PROFILE>/controllers/Rane-One.midi.xml
+
+[Direct Rendering]
+Warned yes
+```
+
+- `[Config] Version` must equal `VersionStore::version()`, so `src/preferences/upgrade.cpp` (line 315) skips every
+  upgrade step and the first-run path. The launcher takes it from `flatpak info org.mixxx.Mixxx` (`Version:
+  2.5.6`) and, once a log exists, from the version line Mixxx logs at startup, so a Flatpak update cannot leave
+  it stale.
+- `[Controller] <name> 1` enables a device; `[ControllerPreset] <name> <file>` binds the mapping
+  (`src/controllers/controllermanager.cpp`: group `[ControllerPreset]`, key `sanitizeDeviceName(name)`, which
+  replaces spaces, `/` and `\` with `_`; absolute mapping paths are accepted by `findMappingFile`).
+- The device name `Midi Through Port-0` is the ALSA port name PortMidi reports; *expected*, M1-05 confirms it
+  from the controller list in `mixxx.log` (if Mixxx names it differently, the key follows the log and Evidence
+  records it). PortMidi devices starting with `MIDI Through Port` are only listed with `--developer`
+  (`src/controllers/midi/portmidienumerator.cpp`, `recognizeDevice`), which the launch command passes.
+- The launcher also **removes** any `[Controller]` key whose name contains `rane` (case-insensitive) or sets it to
+  `0`: the night rule (Mapping skeleton) holds even if someone enabled it in the GUI.
+- Library rescans: the cfg is never saved on a hard kill, so Mixxx rescans the (empty) music directory on every
+  start (`coreservices.cpp`, `SupportedFileExtensions`). Harmless; not suppressed.
+
+`soundconfig.xml` (attribute names from `src/soundio/soundmanagerconfig.cpp`; the output type string is still
+`Master` for Main, per `src/soundio/soundmanagerutil.cpp`). **Launcher-owned:** rewritten before every start,
+because Mixxx saves its sound config back after setup (`mixxxmainwindow.cpp`), and if it ever falls back to
+defaults it picks **JACK** when available (the runtime ships pipewire-jack) or the first ALSA device with two
+outputs, usually a `hw:` device, possibly the Rane ONE (`SoundManagerConfig::loadDefaults`). Neither honours
+`PULSE_SINK`.
+
+```xml
+<!DOCTYPE SoundManagerConfig>
+<SoundManagerConfig api="ALSA" samplerate="48000" latency="5" sync_buffers="2"
+                    force_network_clock="0" deck_count="4">
+  <SoundDevice name="pulse" portAudioIndex="-1">
+    <output type="Master" index="0" channel="0" channel_count="2"/>
+  </SoundDevice>
+</SoundManagerConfig>
+```
+
+- `latency` is a buffer-size index, not ms; `5` is Mixxx's default (about 1024 frames at 48 kHz).
+- `name="pulse"` with `portAudioIndex="-1"` matches by name when the name is unique (the index is then filled in);
+  a name mismatch silently drops the device and leads to the modal "no output" dialog. The name `pulse` and the
+  API string `ALSA` are *expected* from PortAudio in the KDE 6.11 runtime; M1-04 confirms them from the device
+  enumeration Mixxx logs and fixes the template if needed.
+- After the routing check passes, the launcher re-reads `soundconfig.xml`: if Mixxx rewrote it with a different
+  `api` or device name, that is a FAIL (stop, exit 1), even if a stream was seen.
+- No inputs, no headphone output, no Booth. Cue (3–4 on the One) stays unrouted.
+
+## Launcher
+
+`tools/mixxx-dev start|stop|log|status` (bash). The launch command is the one in `CLAUDE.md`:
+
+```
+flatpak run org.mixxx.Mixxx --settings-path ~/.var/app/org.mixxx.Mixxx/dev-profile/ --controller-debug \
+  --controller-abort-on-warning --developer
+```
+
+As the tool actually runs it (D9 adds the routing env, the stream name, the instance fd and the log flush level):
+
+```
+setsid flatpak run --env=PULSE_SINK=mixxx-dev --env=PULSE_PROP_OVERRIDE=application.name=mixxx-dev \
+  --instance-id-fd=3 org.mixxx.Mixxx \
+  --settings-path "$PROFILE" --controller-debug --controller-abort-on-warning --developer \
+  --log-flush-level debug \
+  3>"$PROFILE/mixxx-dev.instance" >"$PROFILE/mixxx-dev.stdout" 2>&1 &
+```
+
+- **`start`:** profile guard (exit 3); refuse (exit 1) if `WAYLAND_DISPLAY` is unset, `tools/null-sink status`
+  says absent, or `aconnect -l` shows `Midi Through` port 0 connected to a client whose name contains `rane`
+  (case-insensitive; Mixxx opens Midi Through's output too, and a subscription would carry it to the controller);
+  exit 0 without a second launch if `mixxx-dev.instance` names a live `org.mixxx.Mixxx` instance. Then write the
+  seed; note the inode of `mixxx.log` (Mixxx rotates the old log to `mixxx.log.1` at startup, so the old file
+  still holds a version line); launch detached (no `--die-with-parent`, so Mixxx outlives the tool's shell);
+  wait until the instance file is non-empty and `flatpak ps --columns=instance,application` lists that id as
+  `org.mixxx.Mixxx`; wait up to 60 s from launch for a **new** `mixxx.log` (different inode) and the version
+  line (as built: on the launch's stdout, not in `mixxx.log`; see D10); run the routing check (≤ 30 s from launch) and the soundconfig re-read; print the instance id. Any failure
+  after launch: `stop`, then exit 1.
+- **Instance tracking:** `flatpak run --instance-id-fd=3` writes the new instance id to fd 3, which is redirected
+  into `$PROFILE/mixxx-dev.instance` (one line, the id). No guessing from a before/after `flatpak ps` diff, so a
+  Mixxx that Michael starts at the same moment can't be confused with ours. **That file is the only thing
+  `stop` ever acts on.** This is how CLAUDE.md's "stop every Mixxx you start (by its pid)" is met: the id maps
+  to the wrapper pid via `flatpak ps --columns=instance,pid`, and `flatpak kill` takes the id.
+- **`stop`:** no instance file, or its id not listed by `flatpak ps --columns=instance,application` as
+  `org.mixxx.Mixxx`: delete the stale file, exit 0, kill nothing. Otherwise `flatpak kill <instance id>`, poll
+  `flatpak ps` for up to 10 s until the id is gone, delete the file. **Never `flatpak kill org.mixxx.Mixxx`**
+  (that would also kill a Mixxx Michael started), never `pkill`/`killall`. The tests' `flatpak` stub exits 99 on
+  `kill org.mixxx.Mixxx`.
+- **Stopping is a hard kill.** Mixxx 2.5 installs no SIGTERM/SIGINT handler (no `signal`/`sigaction` in
+  `src/main.cpp`, `src/coreservices.cpp`, `src/mixxxapplication.cpp`), and COSMIC on Wayland has no
+  command-line way to close a window. So `stop` does **not** run the mapping's `shutdown()` and Mixxx does not
+  save `mixxx.cfg` on the way out (fine: the launcher re-applies its keys). `--log-flush-level debug` flushes
+  every Debug-and-above line, so a killed Mixxx loses nothing from `mixxx.log`. Evidence of `shutdown()` comes
+  from a script hot reload instead (see Mapping skeleton).
+- **`log [n]`:** `tail -n "${n:-50}" "$PROFILE/mixxx.log"`.
+- **`status`:** prints the instance id and `running`, exit 0; or `stopped`, exit 1. Same `org.mixxx.Mixxx` check
+  as `stop`.
+- **Session rule:** every session that starts a Mixxx runs `tools/mixxx-dev stop`, then `tools/null-sink down`
+  if it brought the sink up, before it ends, and says so in its progress entry.
+
+`tools/install-mapping` writes every file in `mapping/` into `$PROFILE/controllers/` (creating the directory),
+**always**, even if identical, each through a temp file in the same directory plus `mv` (atomic rename), so a
+running Mixxx's file watcher never reads a half-written script. Same profile guard (exit 3). It copies; it never
+symlinks (`~/projects` and the worktrees are not visible inside the Flatpak).
+
+Tests (`tests/null-sink.test.mjs`, `tests/install-mapping.test.mjs`, `tests/mixxx-dev.test.mjs`) follow
+`tests/midi-send.test.mjs`: stub `pactl`, `flatpak`, `aconnect` (and `tools/null-sink` via an env override such
+as `MIXXX_DEV_NULL_SINK`) on `PATH` in a temp dir, log every stub invocation to a file the test reads, point
+`MIXXX_DEV_PROFILE` at a temp dir and the `/proc/asound` root at a fixture dir (env override). The `flatpak`
+stub's `run` writes a fake id to fd 3 and a new `mixxx.log` with a version line; its `ps` lists that id as
+`org.mixxx.Mixxx`; the `pactl` stub reports a `mixxx-dev` sink-input. Required cases beyond the feature steps:
+`null-sink down` with a stream on `mixxx-dev` does not call `unload-module`; `start` with a sink-input on
+another sink stops the instance and exits 1; `start` with a stale `mixxx.log` does not count its version line;
+`start` with Midi Through subscribed to a Rane client refuses. No test may start a real Mixxx or load a real
+module.
+
+## Mapping skeleton
+
+M1-05 builds the smallest mapping that proves the loop. Nothing in it sends MIDI.
+
+- `mapping/Rane-One.midi.xml`: `<MixxxControllerPreset mixxxVersion="2.5.0" schemaVersion="1">`, `<info>` with
+  name `Rane One (MK1)`, author, description saying it is unfinished, `<controller id="Rane ONE">`,
+  `<scriptfiles>` listing `rane-one-platter.js` (functionprefix empty) then `Rane-One-scripts.js`
+  (functionprefix `RaneOne`), `<controls/>` empty for now. **No `<outputs>` element, no `<output>` anywhere.**
+- `mapping/rane-one-platter.js`: `var RaneOnePlatter = { ... };` (a plain script: no `import`, `export`,
+  `require`; no `engine`, `midi`, `print`, `console` reference). Node loads it with
+  `vm.runInNewContext(source, ctx)` and reads `ctx.RaneOnePlatter`.
+- `mapping/Rane-One-scripts.js`: `var RaneOne = {}; RaneOne.init = function (id, debugging) {...};
+  RaneOne.shutdown = function () {...};` each logging exactly one line with `console.log`:
+  `RaneOne: init <id>` and `RaneOne: shutdown`. A `RaneOne.guard(fn)` wrapper (try/catch, logs the error once
+  with `console.log` and returns) wraps every handler body, so nothing can throw into Mixxx (CLAUDE.md: input
+  handlers never throw). **No `midi.send*` call anywhere.**
+- **"Loads cleanly"** means: between the new log's first line and `RaneOne: init`, and up to the end of the test,
+  no line at `Warning` or `Critical` level in a `controller.*` category (Mixxx logs scripts under
+  `controller.<lower-cased device name>`, `src/controllers/controller.cpp`) or the `js` category, and no
+  "Controller Mapping Error". `--controller-abort-on-warning` turns every script warning into a JS error
+  (`ControllerScriptEngineBase::m_bAbortOnWarning` → `throwJSError`), so this is a strict test. There is no
+  `ControllerScriptEngine` category to grep for; M1-05 records the real category names it sees under Evidence.
+- **Shutdown evidence:** the legacy script engine watches its script files and reloads them when they change
+  (`controllerscriptenginelegacy.cpp`: `m_fileWatcher` → `reload`; `controllerscriptenginebase.cpp`: `reload`
+  calls `shutdown()` then `initialize()`). So M1-05 proves `RaneOne: shutdown` by running `tools/install-mapping`
+  again while Mixxx runs, then checking the log for at least one `RaneOne: shutdown` followed by `RaneOne: init`,
+  with no error after the last init (several pairs are fine: each watched file can fire). A reload may log a
+  "Failed to watch script file" warning, because it re-adds paths that are still watched; M1-05 confirms and
+  records it, and it is the only warning allowed, only after a hot reload. `tools/mixxx-dev stop` (a hard
+  kill) will not log `shutdown`; the progress entry proposes the matching change to the M1-05 step.
+- **Night rule (D9):** the dev profile binds the mapping **only to `Midi Through Port-0`**. The real Rane ONE
+  port is never enabled or bound in the dev profile until Michael says so in `INBOX.md`, even though the mapping
+  sends nothing: binding it would open the controller's port from an unattended Mixxx. Replays go through
+  `tools/midi-send play <Midi Through client>:0 <file.mid>`, the client number resolved by name from
+  `aconnect -o` (`client 14: 'Midi Through'` today).
+- **Gate hardening (M1-05, test first, the 8 existing tests stay green):** `tools/midi-send play` also refuses
+  (exit 3, unarmed) when `aconnect -l` shows the destination port connected onward to a client whose name
+  contains `rane`. Today it checks only the destination's own name, so a stray `Midi Through → Rane ONE`
+  subscription would carry a replay to the controller.
+- `tests/fixtures/three-notes.mid` (M1-05): SMF format 0, three Note-ons on channel 1 (status `90`) with Note-offs,
+  100 ms apart, written by a few lines of Node in the test helper or committed as a 60-byte file.
+
+## MIDI parser
+
+`tools/lib/midi-parse.mjs` (M1-02): a pure ES module, no I/O, shared by `tools/capture`, `tools/replay`,
+`tools/census` and the tests.
+
+```js
+export function createParser(options) // -> { feed(bytes) -> Message[], reset(), stats }
+export function describe(msg)         // -> string
+```
+
+- `feed(bytes)` accepts a `Uint8Array`, `Buffer` or array of numbers and returns the messages completed by these
+  bytes, in order. State (running status, a partial message, a partial SysEx) carries across calls, so a message
+  may be split anywhere between two feeds. The caller timestamps messages (capture stamps each feed with host
+  µs); the parser does not.
+- **Message shape:** `{ status, data }`: `status` the full status byte (running status resolved, so every message
+  carries its own), `data` an array of data bytes. SysEx: `{ status: 0xF0, data: [payload without F0/F7] }`, plus
+  `truncated: true` when it ended without `F7`.
+- **Channel messages:** 0x80 off, 0x90 on, 0xA0 poly pressure, 0xB0 CC, 0xE0 pitch bend take 2 data bytes; 0xC0
+  program, 0xD0 channel pressure take 1. Note-on with velocity 0 stays `0x9n` (no rewriting: captures must show
+  what the device sent).
+- **Running status:** a data byte with no status in front reuses the last channel status. Set by channel
+  messages; cleared by SysEx and system-common messages (0xF0–0xF7); unaffected by real-time bytes.
+- **Real-time bytes (0xF8–0xFF)** may appear anywhere, including between a status and its data bytes and inside a
+  SysEx. Each is emitted at once as `{ status, data: [] }` and does not disturb the message or SysEx around it.
+- **SysEx:** `F0` starts collecting; `F7` ends it and emits it. Any other non-real-time status byte inside an
+  unfinished SysEx emits it with `truncated: true` and is then processed normally. Payloads are capped at
+  65,536 bytes (then emitted truncated) so a stuck stream cannot grow memory without bound.
+- **System common:** 0xF1 (1 data byte), 0xF2 (2), 0xF3 (1), 0xF6 (0). 0xF4, 0xF5 (undefined) and a stray 0xF7
+  are dropped.
+- **Errors never throw.** Data bytes with no running status and the undefined bytes above are dropped and counted
+  in `stats.dropped`; `stats.messages` counts emitted messages. `reset()` clears state and stats.
+  As built (M1-02): `dropped` counts input bytes that ended up in no message (an abandoned partial message
+  adds all its buffered bytes); a non-byte element (e.g. `2.5`, `0x100`) is counted and also abandons the
+  partial message or SysEx around it, so its neighbours never join into a message that was never sent.
+- `describe(msg)`: channel shown 1-based. `cc ch1 6=92` (for `{status:0xB0,data:[6,92]}`), `on ch1 60=100`,
+  `off ch1 60=0`, `pat ch1 60=12`, `pc ch1 5`, `cp ch1 40`, `pb ch1 <0..16383>` (value `lsb | msb << 7`; centre
+  8192), `sysex 6: 00 20 7f 01 02 03` (length, then hex payload, `…` after 32 bytes, ` truncated` suffix),
+  `clock`, `start`, `continue`, `stop`, `sense`, `reset`, `mtc 0x12`, `spp 1234`, `song 3`, `tune`.
+- Tests (M1-02 steps): at least 10 cases, including the capture check. `captures/2026-10-19-quick/platter.mid`
+  decoded to bytes by a short SMF walk inside the test must parse to exactly 29,323 CC, 29,323 pitch bend and 4
+  note messages (2 on, 2 off; 29,323 + 29,323 + 4 = the 58,650 channel events in the file, recounted by the
+  reviewer).
